WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.919
Film Courage: Ben can you share some tips
that Joe gave you not just about screenwriting

00:00:03.919 --> 00:00:09.230
in general but about really pouring your heart
into the script (pouring your whole life into

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:10.230
it)?

00:00:10.230 --> 00:00:13.809
Ben Hernandez Bray, Writer/Director of EL
CHICANO: One of the main ones I think Joe

00:00:13.809 --> 00:00:15.980
taught me is trust your instincts.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:22.550
That’s one of the biggest things as a writer
and be true to the story, be true to yourself

00:00:22.550 --> 00:00:26.050
and put everything on paper or now these days
on a laptop.

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:32.590
Film Courage: What was that process like for
you [writing EL CHICANO]?

00:00:32.590 --> 00:00:33.841
Ben: It was hard.

00:00:33.841 --> 00:00:35.840
It was tough because the story is so personal.

00:00:35.840 --> 00:00:37.480
It took a long time.

00:00:37.480 --> 00:00:44.020
This has been over 10 years so when you have
something that is related to your family about

00:00:44.020 --> 00:00:49.230
losing a brother and for me also losing a
daughter, every single one of these characters

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:54.890
is someone that I grew up with was related
to my family and also the neighborhood so

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:55.930
it’s tough.

00:00:55.930 --> 00:00:57.230
It’s a process.

00:00:57.230 --> 00:01:01.350
I found myself at times even when I was writing
the memoir after a few pages you get emotional.

00:01:01.350 --> 00:01:04.230
It’s really hard but you’ve got to work
through it.

00:01:04.230 --> 00:01:05.580
And Joe was always there for me.

00:01:05.580 --> 00:01:07.890
Film Courage: Do you two almost have like
a shorthand [version of communication] with

00:01:07.890 --> 00:01:08.890
each other?

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:11.250
Where you already know what he’s going to
say and you’ve already answered it?

00:01:11.250 --> 00:01:12.440
Joe Carnahan, Filmmaker: Yes.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:19.800
I think with any great friendship you develop
a way of speaking to one another, a way of

00:01:19.800 --> 00:01:26.140
interacting and it’s like my brother and
I it’s the same thing.

00:01:26.140 --> 00:01:32.110
We have these kind of inside jokes that we’re
laughing at that other people find obnoxious

00:01:32.110 --> 00:01:35.722
because they don’t know, you know what I
mean?

00:01:35.722 --> 00:01:37.420
Because that shorthand doesn’t exist, they
don’t understand.

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:42.650
But there’s almost 20 years of knowing somebody
and spending time with them so there is that.

00:01:42.650 --> 00:01:48.700
And I think there is a likemindedness in Ben
and I as artists.

00:01:48.700 --> 00:01:51.960
I think we enjoy…we’re striving for the
same things.

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:55.170
We don’t want these kind of artificial experiences.

00:01:55.170 --> 00:01:59.620
We wanted to make EL CHICANO not only something
that was not only singular in its kind of

00:01:59.620 --> 00:02:06.310
vision but also in its unique sense of family,
unique kind of emotional journey that the

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:10.842
character goes on that we both thought was
very necessary and that’s kind of what we

00:02:10.842 --> 00:02:11.890
strove for.

00:02:11.890 --> 00:02:18.030
Film Courage: How usual or unusual is it for
someone to spend 20 years behind the camera,

00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:20.360
then getting their chance to direct.

00:02:20.360 --> 00:02:24.200
Is this something that most people don’t
realize about Hollywood?

00:02:24.200 --> 00:02:30.690
Ben: It’s not unusual because we are storytelling.

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:36.209
Even as early as being a stuntman to then
becoming a stunt coordinator and a second

00:02:36.209 --> 00:02:42.240
unit director for Joe, I was very fortunate.

00:02:42.240 --> 00:02:47.781
I had some people who really took me under
their wing, Ron Stein being one of them who

00:02:47.781 --> 00:02:51.420
was like a father figure when I first started
in the business and it was like you can only

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.950
do this for so long as a young stunt guy you
know?

00:02:53.950 --> 00:02:54.960
It’s like being an athlete.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:03:00.099
If you really want to have longevity I think
you better learn lenses and learn story.

00:03:00.099 --> 00:03:04.300
Of course I fell in love with the fact of
being a storyteller.

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:10.230
In same way you are always directing, you
are always coming up with ideas and with the

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:15.490
relationship I had with Joe it’s like this
collaboration of when he was doing whether

00:03:15.490 --> 00:03:22.080
it be the A-TEAM or THE GREY some of these
films, sitting down and talking about storyboards

00:03:22.080 --> 00:03:24.900
and what needs to be shot it was like you
were in school.

00:03:24.900 --> 00:03:29.360
Film Courage: We have a lot of screenwriters
that watch our channel.

00:03:29.360 --> 00:03:33.800
What do you think is the number one thing
screenwriters do to ruin a screenplay that

00:03:33.800 --> 00:03:35.080
they need to work on?

00:03:35.080 --> 00:03:42.860
Joe: I think my thought on that Karen is they
spend too much time over analyzing it.

00:03:42.860 --> 00:03:48.319
I think that hopeful this new generation of
writers is moving further away from the kind

00:03:48.319 --> 00:03:56.110
of the…let’s call it the Robert McKee
story and you know what I would find arguably

00:03:56.110 --> 00:04:02.720
the dearth of good writing is to try to plug
it into this formula this must happen at this

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:03.720
page.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.710
That must happen at that page because I’ll
find you an example throughout the history

00:04:06.710 --> 00:04:13.019
of screenplays where you can confound whatever
it is it’s put out there and I find that

00:04:13.019 --> 00:04:18.959
very almost dangerously homogenizing a process
which is by its very nature spontaneous and

00:04:18.959 --> 00:04:24.099
creative and should be…I think when a writer
stops being spontaneous or stops allowing

00:04:24.099 --> 00:04:28.120
things to happen to the story, it’s when
it’s dangerous.

00:04:28.120 --> 00:04:34.629
Because in doing it also siphons off the individual
nature of whomever that writer is and when

00:04:34.629 --> 00:04:42.000
you see it done someone like Charlie Kaufman
who writes masterful, very unusual, very specific

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.659
screenplays that’s why he’s regarded in
the way that he is.

00:04:45.659 --> 00:04:48.991
I think you’ve got to keep that in mind
and never lose sight of the fact that you

00:04:48.991 --> 00:04:52.819
are an individual and that screenplay should
live and breathe in you.

00:04:52.819 --> 00:04:54.860
Film Courage: Excellent, thank you!

